# Byte-exact checkout on every platform, by default, for every path.
#
# This pack's evidence is byte-exact. Islands ship fixtures that are deliberately CRLF, that
# carry a BOM, that are Latin-1, and that are not valid UTF-8 at all — each one exists to prove a
# gate handles that shape. Git's line-ending conversion is therefore not a convenience here; it
# is corruption of the evidence. And the reverse bites too: a clone with the Windows default
# `core.autocrlf=true` rewrote all 19 shell files to CRLF, 14 of them then failed `bash -n`, and
# three headline gates turned red on a tree that is provably green — a false red produced by the
# reader's git config rather than by anything in the repository.
#
# `-text` on every path tells Git to store and check out these bytes unchanged, whatever
# `core.autocrlf` / `core.eol` the reader has set. It is the repository-level control Git
# documents for exactly this, applied at the only scope that is safe when both LF files and
# deliberately-CRLF fixtures live in the same tree: all of it.
#
# Adding a normal `text eol=lf` rule for source and enumerating the fixtures as exceptions was
# considered and rejected — it makes every future fixture a landmine that silently normalises
# unless someone remembers to exempt it, and a silent normalisation is precisely the failure this
# file exists to prevent.
* -text

# These two regression fixtures intentionally carry CRLF bytes. Tell `git diff --check` that
# their carriage returns terminate lines rather than constituting trailing whitespace.
skills/qa-script-seat/scripts/fixtures/clean-bound-gate-crlf.py whitespace=cr-at-eol
skills/qa-script-seat/scripts/fixtures/dirty-crlf-gate.sh whitespace=cr-at-eol
